// ============================================================
// RHYTHM
// What harmony.js is to pitch, this is to time. The circle of fifths is
// generated by adding 7 semitones mod 12 over and over; a Euclidean rhythm
// E(k, n) is generated by adding n/k steps mod n over and over, and produces
// the maximally even set — the same object on a different circle. E(3,8) is the
// tresillo, E(5,16) the bossa clave, E(8,12) the shuffle, and E(7,12) is the
// major scale itself. That is not a metaphor; it is the same arithmetic.
//
// So the half of a groove that keeps time is *generated*, because in every one
// of these traditions the timeline pattern genuinely is maximally even. The
// backbone — where the kick and snare land — is *authored*, because that is the
// style and there is no formula for it. tests/rhythm.test.mjs then holds the
// generated half against the definition and the authored half against its own
// invariants, with nothing checking itself.
//
// Like harmony.js this file imports nothing, on purpose: tests/load-module.mjs
// can only load a module that stands alone, and this is the half of the groove
// wheel worth checking in Node.
// ============================================================

// The parts the audio path can actually play. Held against the branches in
// js/play/loop.js by the test — an unhandled name there used to fall through to
// a silent 120 Hz tom rather than to an error.
export const KIT_PARTS = ['kick', 'snare', 'hihat', 'hihatOpen', 'tom1', 'tom2', 'floor', 'crash'];

// One character per step. A rest, a ghost note, a normal hit, an accent — the
// four dynamics a drummer plays and the reason a pattern here does not sound
// like a machine. A string is also the only format where the evenness is
// visible in the source: 'x--x--x-' reads as spread out, [0,3,6] does not.
export const STEP_CHARS = { '-': null, o: 0.42, x: 0.74, X: 1 };

// ============================================================
// EUCLIDEAN RHYTHM
// onset(i) = ceil(i * steps / onsets) places `onsets` hits as evenly as whole
// steps allow: every gap is floor(steps/onsets) or ceil(steps/onsets), so the
// gaps take at most two values and they differ by one. That *is* the definition
// of maximally even, so it needs no lookup table and no Bjorklund recursion.
//
// `rotation` turns the necklace, left by that many steps. The generated set
// always starts on a hit; a rotation is how the named patterns of a tradition
// are reached, since which hit a culture calls "one" is a choice the arithmetic
// does not make. E(5,8) rotated twice is the cinquillo; E(5,16) rotated ten
// times is the bossa clave; E(7,12) rotated seven times is the major scale.
// ============================================================
export function euclid(onsets, steps, rotation = 0) {
  const hits = new Array(steps).fill(false);
  if (!(onsets > 0) || !(steps > 0)) return hits;
  const count = Math.min(onsets, steps);
  const placed = new Array(steps).fill(false);
  for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) placed[Math.ceil((i * steps) / count)] = true;
  for (let i = 0; i < steps; i++) hits[i] = placed[(i + rotation % steps + steps) % steps];
  return hits;
}

export function euclidPattern(onsets, steps, rotation = 0) {
  return euclid(onsets, steps, rotation).map((hit) => (hit ? 'x' : '-')).join('');
}

/** '--o-X---' → [{ step: 2, vel: 0.42 }, { step: 4, vel: 1 }] */
export function parseStepPattern(text) {
  const hits = [];
  for (let step = 0; step < text.length; step++) {
    const vel = STEP_CHARS[text[step]];
    if (vel) hits.push({ step, vel });
  }
  return hits;
}

// ============================================================
// THE LIBRARY
// Twelve grooves, so the wheel's wedges are 30° and the chord wheel's geometry
// carries over unchanged. Four families of three, and both axes are position:
//
//   * The family boundaries land on 12 / 3 / 6 / 9 o'clock, which are also the
//     four beats of the bar on the ring inside — the two circles share their
//     heavy spokes instead of fighting over them.
//   * Clockwise inside a family is strictly busier. A wedge's position
//     therefore predicts what it will sound like, which is the whole reason the
//     layout is worth its corner of the screen.
//
// `beats` is how many pulses the steps group into. Every groove here is in
// four; the field exists so a 3/4 family is a data change rather than a
// rewrite, since the same twelve wedges regrouped is a genuinely interesting
// thing to watch happen.
//
// `timeline` declares which parts are generated and from what. The pattern
// strings below still spell those parts out, so the source shows the shape —
// the test holds the two against each other, which is what catches a hand edit
// that quietly breaks the evenness.
// ============================================================
